        "original_id": "9513d1e6-679f-52a3-a37e-26e7f6b9a5b3",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Clement Greenberg, Agatha Christie, Joseph Drapell, Jack Bush\n- Organizations: Cranbrook Academy, Old Bailey\n- Works: Witness for the Prosecution, Witness for the Prosecution (play), Witness for the Prosecution (1957 film)\n- Roles: art critic, essayist, novelist, short story writer, playwright, artist\n- Topics: American Modern art, murder trial\n\nFacts:\n- Agatha Christie, born on 15 September 1890 and died on 12 January 1976, was an English crime novelist, short story writer, and playwright.\n- Clement Greenberg, born on January 16, 1909 and died on May 7, 1994, was an American essayist and influential visual art critic associated with mid-20th-century American Modern art.\n- Clement Greenberg occasionally wrote under the pseudonym K. Hardesh.\n- At Cranbrook Academy, Joseph Drapell met visiting Canadian artist Jack Bush and American art critic Clement Greenberg, and both influenced his work.\n- The 1957 film Witness for the Prosecution is set in the Old Bailey in London.\n- The 1957 film Witness for the Prosecution is based on Agatha Christie's play of the same name.\n- The 1957 film Witness for the Prosecution concerns the trial of a man accused of murder.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
